"executive_summary": "This paper establishes a phenomenological theory for the anomalous Hall effect (AHE) in magnets, specifically focusing on electric-field-driven AHE arising from nonlinear magnetoelectric coupling. While linear magnetoelectric coupling has been studied, the role of nonlinear terms remains largely unexplored. Using group-theoretical symmetry analysis, the authors demonstrate that a wide variety of magnetic point groups (MPGs) host AHE driven by uni-axial, bi-axial, or tri-axial electric fields. A key finding is that these electric-field-induced Hall conductivities are reversible by reversing the magnetic order parameter, a consequence of time-reversal symmetry and Onsager reciprocity. The study identifies 90 type-I and type-III MPGs that support such effects. To validate the theory, first-principles calculations are performed on two candidate materials: Cr2O3 and CoF2. For Cr2O3, the calculations show that σzy, σxz, and σyx conductivities are driven by Ex, Ey, and Ez respectively, with values exceeding 100 S/cm at 4 MV/cm. For CoF2, the conductivities respond via second-order coupling, with σyx reaching over 200 S/cm. The work highlights the potential of nonlinear magnetoelectric coupling for probing magnetic orders in spintronic applications, offering a new avenue for data readout in memory devices.",
